  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1246 PM MDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nHigh pressure anchored over the southeast CONUS will continue to\nblock eastward progression of the troughing over the West this\nweek. A resident jet streak will continue to provide upper level\nsupport for shower and storm development. Moisture supplies\nbecome the pivotal variable in the equation as the week\nprogresses. The CWA remains under a marginal risk for excessive\nrainfall as well. With the lower atmosphere sufficiently moist,\nstorms should have no problem producing measurable rainfall this\nweek. This will continue to pose a flash flood risk around the\nregion. Guidance continues to suggest the best moisture will\nfocus on the Four Corners region, holding south of I-70.\n\nThis afternoon, storms will continue to take advantage of the\noverhead jet, which was sampled well on the morning Grand\nJunction sounding. Convective parameters remain bullish on the\nsounding too. Storms have been training southwest to northeast\nthus far today. Flash flood prone locales will remain a concern\nshould we see sufficient storm training this afternoon and this\nevening. Hi res models are keeping shower activity lively again\nthrough the overnight hours. With little opportunity to dry \nback out, Tuesday will see the return of flash flooding risk. \nHigh pressure nudges back westward, allowing some richer \nsubtropical moisture to get entrained into the upper level flow \non Tuesday afternoon. Much of southeast UT and southwest CO get \nupgraded into a Slight Risk area for flash flooding on Tuesday. \n\nThe synoptic pattern sees little change as the week progresses.\nSubtropical moisture remains persistent and forcing aloft will\nsustain thunderstorms each day. The flash flood risk remains in\nplay Wednesday and Thursday, as we never really dry out. On\nFriday, models are trying to eject the low out of the PACNW,\nwith a transitional ridge behind it perhaps drying us out a\nsmidge...or at least suppressing the subtropical moisture tap\nenough to limit rainfall. Forecast confidence wanes rather\nquickly this weekend, as models are trying to grapple with the\npattern shift. While it does look like moisture does contract,\nthe upper level pattern looks busy enough to keep some\norographic showers present through the extended period of this\nforecast. With the Vernal Equinox approaching quickly and\ndaylight fading, it seems apropos to discuss fall weather.",
